  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 PM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nNo major changes made to the forecast with a persistence type \nforecast looking to most likely work out as the overall weather \npattern across the United States remains locked in place. This will \nlead to hot afternoons with some diurnally driven convection firing \noff across the Cumberland Plateau and southern Appalachian \nMountains... Much like they're currently doing this afternoon.\n\nIt'll remain a copy/paste forecast with afternoon Heat Index's near \nor just above the triple digit mark for much of the eastern \nTennessee Valley. The hottest temperatures of the 7-day forecast \nappear to be Wednesday and Thursday, when the ambient temperatures \nare highest, as well as an increase in humidity. It's possible a \nHeat Advisory could be introduced for a few counties later in the \nweek, especially for portions of the mid and southern valley. \nTemperatures compared to climatology, will be running around 10 \ndegrees above normal.\n\nHeading into the weekend we'll finally have a chance at a pattern \nchange as a stronger trough will try and suppress the ridge. At the \nsurface, a low pressure center and cold front extending out from it \nwill try to move in from the north. By the end of the first weekend \nof September, the ridge becomes centered near the Lee of the \nRockies. Moisture from the Gulf also tries to move north the same \ntime of the front edging south, which may become the next time the \nforecast area sees chances of widespread precipitation. Only the \nhigher terrain will see differential heating induced showers or \nstorm the next few afternoons. Temperatures will also trend cooler \nover the weekend and into next week, with much lower heights, \nnorthwest flow, and increasing precipitation chances. Humidity \nshould also drop with this system as well.",
